WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.012
Window averages let us show detail and context together.

00:00:04.012 --> 00:00:11.402
In this example, every employee row remains, while additional columns show the average salary for that

00:00:11.402 --> 00:00:15.397
employee's department and the average salary for the entire company.

00:00:15.397 --> 00:00:19.382
Run the query and look at two employees from the same department.

00:00:19.382 --> 00:00:24.887
Their department-average value should be the same, because the window partition is the same,

00:00:24.887 --> 00:00:28.172
while their individual salaries remain different.

00:00:28.172 --> 00:00:34.492
Then compare those department averages with the overall company average.

00:00:34.492 --> 00:00:40.742
This kind of result is useful when you want to evaluate each individual row relative to a peer

00:00:40.742 --> 00:00:46.772
group or to the entire dataset without losing the original detail.

00:00:46.772 --> 00:00:49.287
Always name the thing being counted or averaged.

00:00:49.287 --> 00:00:52.832
COUNT star counts rows, COUNT of a column ignores NULL in that column,

00:00:52.832 --> 00:00:57.872
and COUNT DISTINCT answers a different question again.

00:00:57.872 --> 00:01:04.412
In dashboards, a vague label such as Count can hide major analytical errors; use labels such as admissions,

00:01:04.412 --> 00:01:06.720
patients, or distinct orders.
